% ============================================================================
% eye-parts — what each part of the human eye does, in the source's own words
% (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A vision-anatomy fact on the way to medicine: light travels through the eye in
% order, and each part it passes has ONE job. The CORNEA bends the incoming light;
% the IRIS controls how much light gets through; the PUPIL is the opening the
% light gets in by; the LENS focuses that light onto the back of the eye; the
% RETINA turns the light into electrical signals; and the OPTIC NERVE carries
% those signals to the brain. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? eye_part_function(retina, $F)     % turns_light_into_signals
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `eye_part_function(part, function)` carrying the table's citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% function WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on
% anything that is not one of the listed eye parts (it never invents a function).
%
% The path of sight, as a little truth table (part → the job the source states):
%
%     part          function                 what the source says it does
%     -----------   ----------------------   -------------------------------------
%     cornea        bends_light              dome-shaped front that bends light
%     pupil         lets_in_light           the opening light enters the eye by
%     iris          controls_light          sets how much light the pupil lets in
%     lens          focuses_light           focuses light onto the retina
%     retina        turns_light_into_signals turns light into electrical signals
%     optic_nerve   carries_signals_to_brain carries the signals to the brain
%
% Because each `function` is a stable token, a recalled function composes straight
% into checks: a note that says the retina "focuses light" is suspect precisely
% because focusing is the LENS's job, and the retina's job is turning light into
% signals; a note that has the optic nerve "sensing light" contradicts this table.
% That is the concrete bridge from anatomy RECALL to reasoning, and the rung a
% medicine agent climbs UP from before it can reason about where vision breaks (a
% clouded lens is a focusing problem; a damaged retina or optic nerve is a
% signal problem). The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a function and recall the
% part that does it:
%
%     ? eye_part_function($P, focuses_light)  % lens
%
% Something that is not one of the listed eye parts — the eardrum, the sclera, a
% rock — has no row, so a recall on it ABSTAINS rather than inventing a function.
% That honest-abstention property is what makes the table safe to reason from.
%
% The `function` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to stay as
% close as possible to the verb the source EXPLICITLY uses for that part (the
% source says the cornea "bends light", not "refracts"; the retina "turn[s] the
% light into electrical signals"), so every value stays literally checkable
% against the quoted span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every part→function pair was
% read out of a single fetched U.S. government page this session, and any pair
% that could not be grounded there would have been dropped). The single source is
% the primary U.S. National Institutes of Health reference, the National Eye
% Institute (NEI), "How the Eyes Work" (nei.nih.gov), whose step-by-step
% description of how light moves through the eye states each part's job. Each
% span is quoted here so any reader can re-check it, character-for-character
% except for comment line-wrapping and the U+00A0 no-break spaces noted below:
%
%   cornea → bends_light
%     "The cornea is shaped like a dome and bends light to help the eye focus."
%
%   pupil → lets_in_light
%     "Some of this light enters the eye through an opening called the pupil
%      (PYOO-pul)."
%     Its function takes TWO spans -- that one says light enters through the
%     pupil, and the IRIS sentence says what the pupil lets in is controlled.
%     The row therefore carries the iris sentence as a row-level `cites`, the
%     way `geography/reference-lines.adj` does for a row whose warrant needs two
%     spans. Before that, this header said the two were "fixed together" while
%     the row shipped only one, so the prose and the machine value disagreed.
%
%   iris → controls_light
%     "The iris (the colored part of the eye) controls how much light the pupil
%      lets in."
%
%   lens → focuses_light
%     "The lens works together with the cornea to focus light correctly on the
%      retina."
%
%   retina → turns_light_into_signals
%     "When light hits the retina (a light-sensitive layer of tissue at the back
%      of the eye), special cells called photoreceptors turn the light into
%      electrical signals."
%
%   optic_nerve → carries_signals_to_brain
%     "These electrical signals travel from the retina through the optic nerve to
%      the brain."
%
%   (The retina and optic-nerve sentences carry U+00A0 NO-BREAK SPACES on the
%   page, which a comment cannot show; see the measurement note below.)
%
% (The sclera, macula, and the aqueous/vitreous humors are NOT described on this
% page, so they are deliberately not rows — the table states only the six parts
% whose function the source spells out.)
%
% EVERY ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SPAN, below, in the row's `{ ... }` block
% (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e). Six rows, six distinct sentences, ONE page -- so no row
% carries a `locator` and every row inherits the envelope's, which is the form
% RS-5e's own example uses.
%
% WHAT #14986 CHANGED. The CORNEA sentence was this table's `source` -- the
% field that carries the tier -- for all six rows, so
% `? eye_part_function(optic_nerve, $F)` came back proved by "The cornea is
% shaped like a dome and bends light to help the eye focus."
%
% EVERY SENTENCE IS ON THE SAME PAGE, so the locator was never wrong here and a
% hostname assertion could never have caught this -- only the span was.
%
% BLOCK STRUCTURE WAS CHECKED BEFORE ANY SPAN WAS CUT: the page has TEN `<p>`
% and twenty-nine `<li>`, and inside `<main>` it has FOUR `<p>` and ZERO `<li>`.
% Every sentence used here comes from one of three narrative `<p>` paragraphs
% tracing the path light takes; the `<li>` blocks are site navigation.
%
% (An earlier wording said SIX `<p>`. That number came from counting `<p ` --
% with a trailing space -- which misses every BARE `<p>`, and the three
% narrative paragraphs are bare. The conclusion held; the measurement offered
% as its warrant did not.) Sentence-level spans are legitimate here, measured rather
% than assumed. (The check exists because flattened text hides the difference --
% on the SEER cell-cycle page a span that read as a cut sentence turned out to
% be a complete paragraph followed by an ordered list.)
%
% THE ENVELOPE IS A FRAMING SPAN: the page's own opening sentence. It names NO
% part -- checked against all six row keys -- so it warrants none of the six
% rows.
%
% THREE SENTENCES NAME MORE THAN ONE PART, which is why "the span mentions the
% key" is not on its own a sufficient pairing test: the LENS sentence names the
% cornea and the retina, the OPTIC-NERVE sentence names the retina, and the
% IRIS sentence names the pupil. Giving the cornea row the lens sentence would
% pass a mention test; so would giving the pupil row the iris sentence, and
% that is the sharpest case here because the pupil's own span is the weakest of
% the six. All are caught by the source-equality pin, and the swaps are
% mutants.
%
% (An earlier wording said TWO and omitted the iris/pupil overlap -- the one
% that matters most.)
%
% All seven spans (six warrants plus the framing envelope) were said to be
% confirmed verbatim, each occurring EXACTLY ONCE. Measured 2026-09-15 on the
% raw page (HTTP 200; a nonsense path on the same host returns 404), with
% inline tags removed without a space and only ASCII whitespace collapsed,
% FIVE held. The retina and optic-nerve sentences occurred ZERO times: the
% page writes U+00A0 (NO-BREAK SPACE) where they had ordinary spaces, twice
% in the retina sentence (around "retina") and three times in the optic-nerve
% sentence (after "the", "optic" and "nerve"). With the page's characters
% each occurs exactly once, and both rows now carry them (#14986). All six
% row spans are distinct.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table eye_part_function {
    columns part, function

    % Each row carries the sentence that states ITS OWN job, from the
    % three narrative paragraphs on the one NEI page that trace the
    % path light takes. Six rows, six distinct sentences. Until
    % #14986 every row carried the CORNEA sentence.
    row (cornea, bends_light) {
        source "The cornea is shaped like a dome and bends light to help the eye focus."
    }
    row (pupil, lets_in_light) {
        source "Some of this light enters the eye through an opening called the pupil (PYOO-pul)."
        cites "The iris (the colored part of the eye) controls how much light the pupil lets in." locator "https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/healthy-vision/how-eyes-work"
    }
    row (iris, controls_light) {
        source "The iris (the colored part of the eye) controls how much light the pupil lets in."
    }
    row (lens, focuses_light) {
        source "The lens works together with the cornea to focus light correctly on the retina."
    }
    row (retina, turns_light_into_signals) {
        source "When light hits the retina (a light-sensitive layer of tissue at the back of the eye), special cells called photoreceptors turn the light into electrical signals."
    }
    row (optic_nerve, carries_signals_to_brain) {
        source "These electrical signals travel from the retina through the optic nerve to the brain."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE IS A FRAMING SPAN: the page's own opening sentence. It
    % names NO part — checked against all six row keys — so it warrants
    % none of the six rows.
    %
    % NO ROW CARRIES A LOCATOR: all six sentences are on this one page, so
    % every row inherits the envelope's, which is what ADJ-TABLES RS-5e's
    % own example does. (#15197 asks whether the cascade should settle on
    % that form everywhere; this table takes it because the page is one.)
    source "All the different parts of your eyes work together to help you see."
    locator "https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/healthy-vision/how-eyes-work"
    trust authoritative
}
